---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Agent, Plan & Ask Modes - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="agent-plan-ask">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        Most AI coding tools give you more than one way to interact with them. Understanding the different modes can help you quickly get the results you want.
      </p>

      <p>
        Three core interaction styles show up across most tools:{" "}
        <strong>Agent</strong>, <strong>Plan</strong>, and <strong>Ask</strong>. How each tool surfaces them differs. Cursor exposes all three
        as distinct modes you switch between (plus a fourth, Debug). Claude Code is a bit different: agentic work is just the default way you talk to it,
        Plan is a named mode you toggle into, and there is no separate "Ask" mode; you simply ask read-only questions and it answers without editing. The
        concepts below apply either way, even where the naming doesn't line up exactly.
      </p>

      <h3>Agent Mode</h3>

      <p>
        Agent mode is the fully autonomous option. You describe what you want, and the AI goes off and does it: reading files, editing code, running commands,
        fixing errors it encounters along the way. It's the most powerful mode and the one you'll use for most actual development work.
      </p>

      <p>
        In Cursor, this is the default mode in the Agents window. In Claude Code, it's the standard interaction you get when you type a request in the terminal.
      </p>

      <p>
        Agent mode works best when you have a clear, well-scoped task. "Add a POST endpoint for creating users that validates the email format and returns the
        new user object" is a great agent mode prompt. "Make the app better" is not. Ideally you are referencing requirements and/or tickets to help the AI
        understand the task, using integrations or copy/pasting the entire document.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
        <p>
          When using agent mode, keep your tasks direct and to the point. If you find yourself writing a paragraph-long prompt without referencing requirements
          and/or tickets, that's a sign you should either break it into separate tasks or switch to plan mode first.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Plan Mode</h3>

      <p>
        Plan mode is for when the task is big enough that you want to review the approach before any code gets written. Instead of jumping straight into edits,
        the AI researches your codebase, asks clarifying questions, and produces a step-by-step implementation plan you can review and adjust.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cursor will often suggest plan mode for you when it detects a complex task or thinks you haven't provided enough detail. (Claude Code doesn't auto-switch
        into plan mode; it asks clarifying questions instead.) Either way this isn't reliable, so always check the output to see if it's what you expected.
      </p>

      <p>When you don't have detailed requirements and/or tickets, you can use plan mode to help create them.</p>

      <h3>Ask Mode</h3>

      <p>
        Ask mode is read-only. The AI searches your codebase and answers questions without making any changes. This is genuinely one of the most underused and
        most powerful modes available.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask mode is your learning tool. When you join a new project, you can interrogate the codebase: "Where is the routing configured?" "What pattern does
        this project use for API error handling?" "Walk me through what happens when a user submits the checkout form." The AI reads the actual code and gives
        you answers grounded in what's really there, not generic advice.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="example" title="Example: learning a new codebase">
        <p class="font-mono">
          "I'm new to this project. Can you explain how the notification system works? Where are notifications triggered, how do they get delivered, and where
          are the templates stored?"
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        This is also valuable before you start coding. Spend two minutes in ask mode understanding the area you're about to change, then switch to agent or plan
        mode to actually make the changes. You'll write better prompts because you already understand the landscape and what files to reference, and the AI will
        produce better code because your prompts are more specific.
      </p>

      <h3>Cursor's Debug Mode</h3>

      <p>
        Cursor also offers a dedicated <strong>Debug</strong> mode for tracking down difficult bugs. Instead of immediately trying to fix the issue, it generates
        hypotheses, adds log statements, and uses runtime information to pinpoint the root cause before making a targeted fix. This is particularly useful for race
        conditions, performance issues, and regressions where the obvious fix isn't the right one.
      </p>

      <h3>Choosing the Right Mode</h3>

      <p>Here's a simple decision framework:</p>

      <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
        <div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
          <div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-2">Quick reference</div>
          <ul class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li>
              <strong>Ask</strong> &rarr; "I need to understand something before I start"
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Plan</strong> &rarr; "I don't have requirements, and the task is non-trivial"
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Agent</strong> &rarr; "I know exactly what I want and the task is well-scoped"
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Debug</strong> (Cursor) &rarr; "Something is broken and I'm not sure why"
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Callout variant="warning" title="Common mistake">
        <p>
          Defaulting to agent mode for everything. The other modes have well built prompts under the hood to have the AI best accomplish the task at hand.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Further Reading</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 my-4">
        <li>
          <a href="https://cursor.com/blog/plan-mode" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introducing Plan Mode</a> — Cursor's official write-up on how plan mode works
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://cursor.com/changelog/2-2" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Debug Mode (Cursor 2.2 changelog)</a> — where Cursor introduced Debug mode
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://cursor.com/blog/agent-best-practices" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Best practices for coding with agents</a> — Cursor's official guidance on agent workflows
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code: Permission modes</a> — Anthropic's docs covering Manual, Accept edits, Plan, and Auto modes
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
